report uncertainty appropriate to the scientific claim.

3.27 Professor’s Challenge: When Mathematics Misleads
A research team compares two classifiers for predicting a health outcome.
Model A has an accuracy of 0.91.
Model B has an accuracy of 0.88.
The team concludes:

“Model A is mathematically superior and should therefore be used.”

Critique the conclusion.
Your analysis should address:

outcome prevalence;
sampling variation;
uncertainty in the performance estimates;
sensitivity and specificity;
calibration;
asymmetric error costs;
subgroup performance;
external validity;
whether the same test observations were used for both models;
whether preprocessing was fitted only on training data;
whether a three-percentage-point difference is practically meaningful;
whether mathematical superiority can be defined by a single metric.

Conclude by explaining what additional evidence is required before choosing between the models.

