17.4 Responsibility Is a System Property
Responsible ML cannot be achieved by choosing a “fair algorithm.” Responsible-AI frameworks and sociotechnical scholarship emphasize that risk emerges across the full system lifecycle rather than from the model in isolation (National Institute of Standards and Technology 2023; Selbst et al. 2019).
The system includes:

problem formulation;
data collection;
target definition;
feature construction;
modeling;
thresholding;
interface design;
human decision-makers;
institutional policy;
appeals;
monitoring;
retirement.

Thus:
\[
\boxed{
\text{responsible ML}
\neq
\text{responsible model only}
}
\]

17.6 Map the Stakeholders
Stakeholders may include:

people represented in the data;
people receiving predictions;
decision-makers;
domain professionals;
developers;
institutions;
communities;
regulators;
people indirectly affected by deployment.

The person using the model is not necessarily the person bearing its consequences.

17.32 Procedural Fairness
Even a statistically balanced system may be procedurally unfair if:

decisions are opaque;
people cannot correct erroneous data;
no appeal exists;
responsibility is unclear;
automated recommendations are treated as final.

Responsible systems need legitimate processes, not only metrics.

17.41 Importance Is Not Causality
If feature \(X_j\) is important for prediction, we cannot conclude:
\[
do(X_j)
\rightarrow
Y.
\]
Predictive association can arise from:

confounding;
proxy relationships;
measurement artifacts;
selection;
correlated features.

Therefore:
\[
\boxed{
\text{feature importance}
\neq
\text{causal effect}
}
\]

17.42 Explanations Can Also Mislead
An explanation method can be:

unstable;
sensitive to background data;
incomplete;
difficult for nontechnical users;
persuasive despite being approximate.

An explanation should itself be evaluated for its intended purpose.

17.51 Privacy Is Not Merely a Technical Parameter
Even a privacy-preserving algorithm does not answer:

whether data collection was appropriate;
whether consent was meaningful;
whether the purpose is legitimate;
whether deployment is harmful.

Technical privacy safeguards are necessary in many contexts but not sufficient for ethical legitimacy.

17.53 “Human in the Loop” Is Not Enough
A human reviewer may simply accept model recommendations.
This is known as automation bias.
Meaningful oversight requires that humans have:

relevant expertise;
adequate time;
access to evidence;
authority to disagree;
clear accountability;
mechanisms for escalation.

17.55 Contestability
People affected by consequential predictions may need mechanisms to:

understand the basis of a decision;
correct inaccurate information;
request human review;
appeal outcomes.

Contestability is a design requirement, not an afterthought.

