18.3 Reproducibility Is a Chain
Reproducible research treats the computational workflow as part of the evidence supporting a scientific claim, rather than as disposable implementation detail (Peng 2011; Sandve et al. 2013).
A useful mental model is:
\[
\text{Question}
\rightarrow
\text{Data}
\rightarrow
\text{Environment}
\rightarrow
\text{Code}
\rightarrow
\text{Experiment}
\rightarrow
\text{Model}
\rightarrow
\text{Result}
\rightarrow
\text{Artifact}
\rightarrow
\text{Archive}.
\]
If an important link is missing, reproducing the result becomes harder.

18.9 Final Reflection
A trustworthy machine-learning project requires more than algorithmic performance:
\[
\text{Problem Framing}
+
\text{Measurement}
+
\text{Data}
+
\text{Modeling}
+
\text{Evaluation}
+
\text{Responsibility}
+
\text{Reproducibility}.
\]
Machine learning becomes mature analytical practice when these are treated as one connected system.

18.12 Every Dataset Has a History
Record:

source;
access date;
version or release;
persistent identifier when available;
license;
inclusion/exclusion criteria;
variable definitions;
transformations;
missing-data decisions;
known limitations.

18.16 Random Seeds Improve Repeatability
Many ML procedures contain randomness:

train/test splitting;
bootstrapping;
random forests;
stochastic optimization;
initialization;
augmentation.

A fixed seed can make a computational path easier to reproduce.

rng = np.random.default_rng(
    42
)

rng.normal(
    size=5
)

array([ 0.30471708, -1.03998411,  0.7504512 ,  0.94056472, -1.95103519])

18.17 A Seed Is Not a Reproducibility Guarantee
Results can still differ because of:

software versions;
hardware;
parallel execution;
nondeterministic libraries;
numerical precision;
operating systems;
changed data;
changed defaults.

Therefore:
\[
\boxed{\text{random seed}}
\neq
\boxed{\text{complete reproducibility}}.
\]

18.19 Software Is Part of the Method
The computational environment can affect results.
Record at least:

operating system;
Python version;
package versions;
major external libraries;
hardware when relevant.

18.21 Dependency Files
Common approaches include:

