18.34 Why Use a Remote Repository?
A remote repository can support:

collaboration;
issue tracking;
code review;
version history;
public dissemination;
integration with archival services.

But GitHub itself should not automatically be treated as the permanent scholarly archive.
For a citable release, archive a stable version in an appropriate preservation repository.

18.35 Do Not Commit Secrets
Never commit:

passwords;
API keys;
access tokens;
private credentials;
restricted data.

Use environment variables or secure secret-management systems.
If a secret is committed, deleting the visible line may not remove it from Git history.

18.37 Narrative and Computation Belong Together
Quarto can combine:

prose;
equations;
executable code;
figures;
tables;
citations;
cross-references;
multiple output formats.

This reduces the distance between analysis and publication.

18.41 Exploration and Production Serve Different Purposes
Notebooks are valuable for:

exploration;
teaching;
visualization;
iterative reasoning.

Scripts and modules are often useful for:

reusable transformations;
testing;
automation;
pipelines;
production workflows.

A strong project can use both.

18.45 Serialization Has Limits
Serialized model artifacts can be:

version-dependent;
unsafe to load from untrusted sources;
incomplete without preprocessing and metadata;
incompatible across environments.

Never load an untrusted pickle file.
A model artifact should be accompanied by provenance and environment information.

18.47 Reproducibility Improves When Steps Are Explicit
A workflow might define:
\[
\text{download}
\rightarrow
\text{validate}
\rightarrow
\text{prepare}
\rightarrow
\text{train}
\rightarrow
\text{evaluate}
\rightarrow
\text{render}.
\]
The more these dependencies are automated, the less the workflow depends on undocumented memory.

18.50 Tests Are Not Only for Software Engineers
Research code can test assumptions such as:

IDs are unique;
expected columns exist;
impossible values are absent;
train/test groups do not overlap;
outcome coding is valid;
row counts fall within expected ranges.

18.52 Tests Cannot Prove the Research Question Is Good
Automated tests can verify that code behaves as specified.
They cannot determine whether:

the construct is valid;
the causal claim is justified;
the intervention is ethical;
the research question matters.

Again, computational rigor and scientific judgment are complementary.

18.56 A Repository Is Living; A Release Is Fixed
Active Git repositories evolve.
Scholarly citation often requires a stable snapshot.
A release can freeze a version of:

code;
documentation;
configuration;
figures;
supplementary materials.

18.59 OSF as a Research Hub
OSF can support:

project organization;
registrations;
preregistration;
files;
collaboration;
links to other research services.

The best repository depends on the artifact and research workflow.

18.60 DOI Does Not Mean Peer Reviewed
A DOI establishes persistent identification.
It does not automatically imply:

peer review;
methodological quality;
correctness;
journal publication.

Persistent citation and scientific validation are different functions.

18.62 Make Software and Data Citable
A project can include citation metadata such as:
CITATION.cff
Document:

authors;
title;
version;
release date;
DOI;
repository;
preferred citation.

18.65 Open Science Does Not Mean “Upload Everything”
Open-science norms encourage transparent and reusable research practices while still requiring appropriate protection of sensitive or restricted materials (Nosek et al. 2015).
Health, education, financial, and institutional data may contain sensitive or restricted information.
Responsible reproducibility can use:

data dictionaries;
synthetic examples;
access instructions;
transformation code;
controlled repositories;
metadata;
documented eligibility procedures.

The goal is as open as possible, as restricted as necessary.

18.68 One Analysis Path Is Rarely the Only Reasonable Path
Researchers make choices about:

exclusions;
transformations;
missing data;
features;
models;
hyperparameters;
thresholds;
metrics.

A robustness analysis asks whether conclusions depend heavily on one defensible choice.

18.72 Reproducible Computer Vision: Preserve the Visual Evidence Chain
Computer-vision reproducibility requires more than saving a fitted model.
A visual workflow may depend on:
\[
\text{Raw Image}
\rightarrow
\text{Decode}
\rightarrow
\text{Resize/Crop}
\rightarrow
\text{Normalize}
\rightarrow
\text{Augment}
\rightarrow
\text{Represent}
\rightarrow
\text{Model}
\rightarrow
\text{Evaluate}.
\]
Each transformation can change the evidence.
A reproducible vision project should document, where applicable:

image source and provenance;
licensing and consent constraints;
file formats and decoding libraries;
inclusion and exclusion rules;
duplicate and near-duplicate handling;
subject, device, session, or institution grouping;
resize and crop rules;
channel and color-space handling;
normalization;
augmentation operations and probabilities;
random seeds;
pretrained model or checkpoint identifiers;
model architecture and weights;
train/validation/test source definitions;
evaluation metrics and subgroup analyses;
hardware or accelerator details when they materially affect execution;
generated artifacts and model outputs.


18.72.1 Preserve the Split, Not Just the Seed
For grouped visual data, a random seed alone is insufficient. If images must generalize to new people, devices, or institutions, preserve the group-level split definition or the exact observation identifiers assigned to each partition.
\[
\boxed{\text{Seed}\neq\text{Data-Provenance Record}}
\]


18.72.2 Restricted Images
Visual datasets can be impossible or inappropriate to redistribute because of privacy, consent, licensing, proprietary ownership, or institutional restrictions.
Reproducibility does not require violating those constraints.
Instead, preserve:

acquisition and eligibility logic;
data dictionaries and schemas;
preprocessing code;
checksums or identifiers where permissible;
synthetic or public demonstration data when useful;
access instructions;
documentation of what cannot be shared and why.







NoteBeyond the Algorithm: Reproducibility Preserves How We Saw the Data



In computer vision, preprocessing determines what the model is allowed to see.
Documenting crop rules, image transformations, exclusions, and acquisition conditions therefore preserves more than code. It preserves part of the study’s epistemic viewpoint.




