Guided Self-Study
Self-directed readers can move through the book using four repeating actions:

Understand — read the conceptual and mathematical explanation.
Execute — reproduce the code rather than merely reading it.
Interrogate — change assumptions, parameters, data, or evaluation choices.
Explain — write what the evidence supports, what it does not support, and what you would do next.

A sustainable self-study rhythm is one substantive chapter or chapter pair per week, with additional time for Chapters 3, 5, 13–18 when the material is unfamiliar.

Self-Study Evidence Portfolio
Instead of measuring progress by chapters completed, maintain a small portfolio containing:

reproducible notebooks or Quarto documents;
short explanations of important equations;
model-comparison tables;
error analyses;
one computer-vision investigation;
one responsible-ML audit;
one AI-assisted coding critique;
a final reproducible project.

The portfolio becomes evidence of analytical development rather than a record of pages read.

Teaching Computer Vision Across the Book
Computer vision is intentionally treated as a longitudinal workload. Instructors can use image-based examples to connect:

data representation and preprocessing;
classical classification;
clustering and dimensionality reduction;
neural representations;
multimodal and generative AI;
domain shift and external validation;
fairness, privacy, and responsible deployment;
reproducible visual pipelines.

This approach allows students to experience computer vision without requiring a separate specialized course.

Reproducibility as a Course-Level Expectation
Reproducibility should not first appear in the final week.
From the beginning of a course, students can be expected to:

identify data sources;
preserve provenance;
separate source data from derived data when appropriate;
record transformations;
use deterministic seeds when meaningful;
avoid leakage;
document dependencies;
use version control;
generate results from code;
distinguish exploratory decisions from confirmatory claims.

Chapter 18 then integrates practices students have already experienced throughout the course.

Versioning
The book uses versioned releases so that readers can distinguish the evolving repository from a fixed scholarly release.
The publication record distinguishes among:

the edition of the book;
the version of a specific release;
the Git tag associated with that release;
the archival DOI assigned to the preserved release.

These identifiers serve different purposes.
The Second Edition identifies the intellectual edition of the book. Version 2.0.0 identifies this release. The Git tag v2.0.0 identifies the corresponding repository snapshot. The DOI identifies the archived scholarly object.
The release relationship is:
\[
\text{Second Edition}
\rightarrow
\text{Version 2.0.0}
\rightarrow
\text{Git tag v2.0.0}
\rightarrow
\text{Archived Release}
\rightarrow
\text{DOI 10.5281/zenodo.22136083}.
\]

Repository and Reproducibility
The source files, executable examples, environment information, and reproducibility infrastructure for the Second Edition are maintained with the book repository:
https://github.com/amightyo/machine-learning-using-python-2e
The browsable HTML edition is available at:
https://amightyo.github.io/machine-learning-using-python-2e/
The repository is intended to support:

transparent inspection of the source;
reproducible execution of examples;
version history;
issue tracking and corrections;
preservation of release-specific materials.

Readers reproducing computational results should consult the environment and reproducibility documentation associated with the release they are using.

How to Cite Code or a Specific Release
When reproducibility requires identifying the exact computational state used for an analysis, cite the archived release or version corresponding to that state rather than citing only the continuously updated repository.
A reproducible citation record should make it possible to identify:

the book edition;
the release version;
the repository snapshot or Git tag;
the archival DOI.

This distinction matters because computational dependencies, examples, corrections, and supporting materials can evolve after publication.
